<Base
  title="Psalms 3–4 | Providence Community Church"
  description="A brief reflection on Psalms 3 and 4 — two prayers of David, when he fled from Absalom his son."
  path="/psalmsreflection/"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap" style="max-width:820px;">
      <p class="eyebrow">A morning psalm and an evening psalm</p>
      <h1>I woke again, for the Lord sustained me.</h1>
      <p style="font-family:var(--serif); font-style:italic; color:var(--gray); margin:-6px 0 22px; font-size:1.05rem;">Psalms 3&ndash;4. Of David, when he fled from Absalom his son.</p>
      <p style="font-size:1.12rem;">David wrote these two psalms on the run from his own son. Absalom had stolen the hearts of Israel. Friends had become enemies. And the cruelest word in the whole affair was the one spoken over David&rsquo;s soul: &ldquo;There is no salvation for him in God.&rdquo;</p>
      <p style="font-size:1.12rem;">That is not an ancient insult. It is the oldest one. If God will not help you, nothing else can. Psalm 3 is what a man says when he wakes up in that dark. Psalm 4 is what he says when he lies down in it again.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-rule" id="psalm-3">
    <div class="wrap" style="max-width:820px;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Psalm 3, morning</p>
      <p class="superscription">A Psalm of David, when he fled from Absalom his son.</p>

      <blockquote class="psalm">
        <p>
          <span class="vn">1</span>O LORD, how many are my foes!<br />
          Many are rising against me;<br />
          <span class="vn">2</span>many are saying of my soul,<br />
          there is no salvation for him in God. <em>Selah</em>
        </p>
      </blockquote>
      <p style="font-size:1.12rem;">The first thing David tells the Lord is how many. Not how skilled, not how right they are &mdash; how many. Foes, rising, speaking. The taunt is aimed at his soul, not merely his throne. They are not saying David has made a political mistake. They are saying God has walked away. <em>Selah</em>, the psalm says. Stop there long enough to feel the weight of it.</p>

      <blockquote class="psalm">
        <p>
          <span class="vn">3</span>But you, O LORD, are a shield about me,<br />
          my glory, and the lifter of my head.<br />
          <span class="vn">4</span>I cried aloud to the LORD,<br />
          and he answered me from his holy hill. <em>Selah</em>
        </p>
      </blockquote>
      <p style="font-size:1.12rem;">&ldquo;But you, O Lord.&rdquo; Everything turns on those four words. David does not answer the crowd. He names God: shield, glory, the lifter of my head. A man whose own son has shamed him still has a glory, because the glory is not his reputation. He cried aloud. He was answered from the holy hill &mdash; not from the camp, not from a clever plan, from the place God had set his name.</p>

      <blockquote class="psalm">
        <p>
          <span class="vn">5</span>I lay down and slept;<br />
          I woke again, for the LORD sustained me.<br />
          <span class="vn">6</span>I will not be afraid of many thousands of people<br />
          who have set themselves against me all around.
        </p>
      </blockquote>
      <p style="font-size:1.12rem;">Here is the proof that the prayer was real: he slept. Not because the danger had passed. Thousands were still set against him all around. He slept because the Lord sustained him, and he woke because the Lord sustained him still. Courage in this psalm is not a mood. It is what a man does in the morning when God has kept him through the night.</p>

      <blockquote class="psalm">
        <p>
          <span class="vn">7</span>Arise, O LORD!<br />
          Save me, O my God!<br />
          For you strike all my enemies on the cheek;<br />
          you break the teeth of the wicked.<br />
          <span class="vn">8</span>Salvation belongs to the LORD;<br />
          your blessing be on your people! <em>Selah</em>
        </p>
      </blockquote>
      <p style="font-size:1.12rem;">Then he asks God to do what only God can do &mdash; arise, save, strike, break. David is not taking his own vengeance; he is asking the Judge to judge. And he will not end on his enemies. &ldquo;Salvation belongs to the Lord; your blessing be on your people.&rdquo; The man being hunted still prays for the congregation. That is what faith looks like when it is not performing.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-rule section-panel" id="psalm-4">
    <div class="wrap" style="max-width:820px;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Psalm 4, evening</p>
      <p class="superscription">To the choirmaster: with stringed instruments. A Psalm of David.</p>

      <blockquote class="psalm">
        <p>
          <span class="vn">1</span>Answer me when I call, O God of my righteousness!<br />
          You have given me relief when I was in distress.<br />
          Be gracious to me and hear my prayer!<br />
          <span class="vn">2</span>O men, how long shall my honor be turned into shame?<br />
          How long will you love vain words and seek after lies? <em>Selah</em><br />
          <span class="vn">3</span>But know that the LORD has set apart the godly for himself;<br />
          the LORD hears when I call to him.
        </p>
      </blockquote>
      <p style="font-size:1.12rem;">Evening comes, and the same man is still asking to be heard. He calls God &ldquo;God of my righteousness&rdquo; &mdash; not because David is innocent of every charge, but because his standing is in God. He remembers prior relief: you have given me relief when I was in distress. Past mercy is an argument for present help. And he turns from prayer to the men who love vain words and seek after lies. The Lord&rsquo;s answer to shame is not a better speech. It is this: he has set apart the godly for himself, and he hears.</p>

      <blockquote class="psalm">
        <p>
          <span class="vn">4</span>Be angry, and do not sin;<br />
          ponder in your own hearts on your beds, and be silent. <em>Selah</em><br />
          <span class="vn">5</span>Offer right sacrifices,<br />
          and put your trust in the LORD.
        </p>
      </blockquote>
      <p style="font-size:1.12rem;">&ldquo;Be angry, and do not sin.&rdquo; The psalm does not scold the feeling. It refuses the follow-through. Ponder in your own hearts on your beds, and be silent. Offer right sacrifices. Put your trust in the Lord. The bed is not where we rehearse the insult until it hardens. It is where we stop talking and let God be God.</p>

      <blockquote class="psalm">
        <p>
          <span class="vn">6</span>There are many who say, &ldquo;Who will show us some good?<br />
          Lift up the light of your face upon us, O LORD!&rdquo;<br />
          <span class="vn">7</span>You have put more joy in my heart<br />
          than they have when their grain and wine abound.<br />
          <span class="vn">8</span>In peace I will both lie down and sleep;<br />
          for you alone, O LORD, make me dwell in safety.
        </p>
      </blockquote>
      <p style="font-size:1.12rem;">Many want a sign of good. David asks for a face: &ldquo;Lift up the light of your face upon us, O Lord.&rdquo; Grain and wine can abound, and still the heart can be thin. God has put more joy in him than they have in their harvest. So he will lie down again. &ldquo;In peace I will both lie down and sleep; for you alone, O Lord, make me dwell in safety.&rdquo; Psalm 3 began with a night God got him through. Psalm 4 ends by trusting him for the next one.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-rule">
    <div class="wrap" style="max-width:820px;">
      <p style="font-size:1.12rem;">Two psalms. One flight. Morning and evening, the same confession: salvation belongs to the Lord. The enemies were many. The taunt was sharp. David slept anyway &mdash; not because he was brave, but because he was kept.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

